[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Authentication and Authorization in Microservices (OAuth2, OIDC, JWT)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Securing a distributed microservices architecture requires a robust identity and access management strategy. In a monolithic application, user sessions are often managed via sticky sessions and centralized relational databases. However, microservices require a stateless, scalable, and decentralized approach to security."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts: Authentication vs. Authorization"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Authentication (AuthN): Verifying the identity of a user or service (e.g., Who are you?)."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Authorization (AuthZ): Determining what an authenticated user or service is allowed to do (e.g., What can you access?)."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"The Modern Security Stack: OAuth2, OIDC, and JWT"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"OAuth2 is an authorization framework that enables applications to obtain limited access to user accounts on an HTTP service. OpenID Connect (OIDC) is an identity layer built on top of OAuth2 that handles authentication and provides user profile information using ID Tokens. JSON Web Tokens (JWT) act as the standard compact format for securely transmitting claims between parties."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Token-Based Architecture Flow"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1. The Client sends user credentials to the Authorization Server (Identity Provider)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"2. The Authorization Server validates credentials and returns a signed JWT."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"3. The Client attaches the JWT in the Authorization header (Bearer token) for subsequent requests to Microservices."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"4. Downstream Microservices validate the JWT cryptographically without needing to query a central database."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Validating a JWT in a Microservice"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is a Node.js Express middleware example usingjsonwebtoken and jwks-rsa to validate tokens issued by an OIDC provider like Keycloak or Auth0."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"const express = require('express');
const { expressjwt: jwt } = require('express-jwt');
const jwksRsa = require('jwks-rsa');

const app = express();

const checkJwt = jwt({
  secret: jwksRsa.expressJwtSecret({
    cache: true,
    rateLimit: true,
    jwksUri: 'https://auth.example.com/.well-known/jwks.json'
  }),
  audience: 'https://api.example.com',
  issuer: 'https://auth.example.com/',
  algorithms: ['RS256']
});

app.get('/protected', checkJwt, (req, res) => {
  res.json({ message: 'Access granted!', user: req.auth });
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Microservice running on port 3000'));"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Microservices Security"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use API Gateways to handle authentication at the edge, relieving internal microservices of token validation overhead."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep JWT payload sizes small. Include only necessary claims like user ID, roles, and scopes."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement short-lived access tokens paired with secure refresh token rotation mechanisms."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always enforce HTTPS to prevent token interception during transit."}]
